.TH TKSCENETEST 1
.SH NAME
tkscenetest \- check that libtk's render-node path draws what the direct path draws
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tkscenetest
.RB [ -v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tkscenetest
renders each of a set of cases twice \(em once through
.IR libtk 's
direct path, which paints into the destination image, and once through its
render-node path, which describes the widget as a tree of nodes and renders
the tree \(em and checks that the two produce the same pixels, byte for
byte.
.PP
The cases are one of every widget kind with default options, and then the
widget
.I states
that have node kinds of their own: a selected entry, an entry with
.BR -show ,
a disabled button, a canvas with a rectangle, a line, an oval and a string
in it, a listbox with a selection, a text widget with content, a scale away
from its minimum, a part-scrolled scrollbar, a non-default font and a
sunken frame. Default options alone would pass while every one of those was
broken.
.PP
That identity is the whole acceptance condition for the node tree: a
description that draws something other than what the direct path draws is
not a description of the drawing.
.PP
The path is selected with the
.B render
widget command,
.B direct
or
.BR scene .
.B Direct
is the default, because
.B tkviascene
is zero-initialised, so nothing in a running system takes the node path
unless it is asked for.
.PP
With
.B -v
each passing check is printed as well as each failing one.
.PP
Every comparison is a readback from an image allocated for the purpose,
never from the screen, for the reason
.IR tkofftest (1)
gives: what a widget put on the screen cannot be read back, while an
allocated image can. That is also what lets this run on a kernel with no
display attached \(em it takes a
.B Display
from its
.B Context
when it has one and allocates a bare one when it does not \(em which is the
case it exists for.
.SH "THE TWO CONTROLS, AND WHY A PIXEL COMPARISON NEEDS THEM"
A zero from a predicate is worth nothing until the predicate is shown able
to fire, and an identity check has two distinct ways of passing while
testing nothing.
.TP
.B "the node path never ran"
Identity between the two rounds is exactly what you get if
.B "render scene"
silently did nothing and both rounds took the direct path. So
.B "render count"
is read back and its three figures \(em trees, widgets, nodes \(em must all
be non-zero. A zero there is a failure even when every image matches.
.TP
.B "the comparison cannot see a difference"
A comparison that always reported equality would make every identity check
pass. So two
.I different
widgets are compared as well, and must differ.
.PP
A case may be
.BR skipped ,
counted separately from a pass and a failure, when the environment cannot
distinguish it from the case it is meant to differ from \(em the only such
case is the non-default font, and the Pi 3B kernel's root carries exactly
one font, which is also Tk's default. A case that is dropped is reported,
because coverage quietly lost is worse than coverage never claimed.
.PP
Two further properties of the description are checked, because a tree that
draws correctly can still be useless to a backend: every node that marks
pixels must describe its own box, and the union the trees marked must be a
non-empty rectangle inside the image.
.SH "ONE READ PER ROUND"
.BR "render count" ,
.B "render nodes"
and
.B "render bounds"
share one set of accumulators and
.I each read clears all of them
.RI ( tkwalkreset (\^)
in
.BR libtk/utils.c ).
Reading two in a row gives the second one zero. This program therefore
renders a fresh round before each read, and anything else that asks for
more than one of those figures must do the same.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/tkscenetest.b
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR tkcmd (1)
.SH BUGS
What it does
.B not
cover:
.PP
The widgets are laid out with
.B pack
into a 160\(mu60 image. The states listed above are covered; a
.I focused
widget, a partially-clipped one, a posted menu with a cascade, and a
non-default colour scheme are not, and each reaches node kinds this does
not.
.PP
The "drew something" check counts bytes differing from the white the image
was filled with, so a widget that painted only its own grey background
satisfies it. It is a guard against comparing two blank images, not evidence
that a case's content was drawn; what guards the content is that every
setup command's reply is checked, so a selection or an item that did not
take is reported rather than silently leaving a default widget to be
compared with itself.
.PP
The two controls above rule out the node path not running and the
comparison not working. They do not amount to showing that a
.I deliberate
defect in a scene conversion would be caught; that needs the defect
injected, which cannot be done from here.
.PP
Nothing here looks at the screen, so it says nothing about what the board
displays \(em only about what the two paths compute. A readback cannot see
tearing or a stale buffer at all.
